# [Applications and serving APIs]($heading.id('serving-apis'))

[Applications and agents]($text.id('component-applications')) send HTTP requests to
[LLMD]($text.id('component-llmd')). The serving engine runs the model and the endpoint determines what the client receives.

| Endpoint | Result |
| --- | --- |
| [Chat completions]($link.page('llmd/reference/v1/chat/completions')) · [/v1/chat/completions]($text.id('component-chat-completions')) | An OpenAI-compatible client or SDK sends messages. LLMD returns a completion or streams the generated text. |
| [Embeddings]($link.page('llmd/reference/v1/embeddings')) · [/v1/embeddings]($text.id('component-embeddings')) | The model pools its output on the device and returns a vector for each input text. |
| [Decision]($link.page('llmd/reference/v1/systemone')) · [/v1/systemone]($text.id('component-decision')) | The client supplies one state and a set of yes/no, choice or score questions. A question can depend on an earlier answer. |

For [observability]($text.id('component-observability')), LLMD exposes Prometheus metrics and provides crash reports.
See [Monitor and troubleshoot LLMD]($link.page('llmd/howto/monitoring-and-troubleshooting').ref('scrape-service-metrics')) to inspect service metrics during a run.

# [Serving engine]($heading.id('engine'))

The [engine]($text.id('component-engine')) prepares input, schedules model execution and returns results to clients.

## [Input and output]($heading.id('engine-input-and-output'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-chat-templates">Chat templates</dt>
    <dd>Format messages into the model’s prompt with a Jinja2 engine in Zig.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-tokenizers">Tokenizers</dt>
    <dd>Convert prompts into token IDs using tokenizers such as BPE, WordPiece or SentencePiece.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-output-parsers">Output parsers</dt>
    <dd>Extract reasoning traces and tool calls from generated content.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

## [Scheduling and decoding]($heading.id('engine-scheduling-and-decoding'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-scheduler">Scheduler</dt>
    <dd>Mixes prefill and decode in active batches and splits long prefills into chunks.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-paged-kv-cache">Paged KV cache</dt>
    <dd>Stores attention keys and values in pages for reuse across requests with a shared prefix.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-speculative-decoding">Speculative decoding</dt>
    <dd>Uses a drafter such as DSpark, DFlash or DSpine to propose tokens for the target model to verify.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-async-execution">Async execution</dt>
    <dd>The host prepares the next step while the device runs the current one.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

See [Batching and memory]($link.page('llmd/reference/batching-and-memory')) for scheduler and cache flags,
and [External drafters]($link.page('llmd/reference/model-compatibility').unsafeRef('external-drafters')) for model pairings.

## [Execution setup]($heading.id('engine-execution-setup'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-parallelism">Parallelism</dt>
    <dd>Tensor and expert parallelism split model work across devices, while data parallelism distributes inputs across replicas.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-weight-preparation">Weight preparation</dt>
    <dd>Fuse, stack or swizzle weights in formats such as FP8, MXFP4 and NVFP4 into the layout a kernel expects.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

# [Qwen 3.5 example]($heading.id('qwen-3-5'))

The compact map traces embedding, repeated decoder layers, the LM head and sampling.
In this Qwen 3.5 example, a decoder layer runs RMSNorm, then Gated DeltaNet or attention,
followed by RMSNorm and a mixture of experts (MoE). The reusable components row shows
building blocks shared by other models written in Zig.

# [DeepSeek V4.1-Flash example]($heading.id('deepseek-v4-1-flash'))

Follow a token through [DeepSeek V4.1-Flash]($text.id('component-model')).

1. **[Embedding.]($text.id('component-embedding'))** Look up a vector for each input token ID.
2. **[Decoder layer × N.]($text.id('component-decoder-layer'))** Pass the vectors through the repeated decoder layers. The table below expands one layer.
3. **[LM head.]($text.id('component-lm-head'))** Project the decoder output to scores over the token vocabulary.
4. **[Sampling.]($text.id('component-sampling'))** Select the next token using those scores.

The optional [DSpark drafter]($text.id('component-dspark-drafter')) uses one decoder layer to propose tokens for the target model to verify.

| Inside a decoder layer | Role |
| --- | --- |
| [Engram]($text.id('component-engram')) | On selected layers, the host computes n-gram hashes for lookups in host-pinned tables. A gate controls how much of the result is added to the residual stream. |
| [Sparse MLA attention]($text.id('component-sparse-mla-attention')) | Latent query and KV projections use YaRN rotary embeddings. A KV compressor and top-k indexer support sliding-window and sparse attention with attention sinks, followed by a grouped output projection. |
| [Mixture of experts]($text.id('component-mixture-of-experts')) | A router selects the top-k MXFP4 routed experts. The layer also has a shared expert and can distribute experts across devices. |
| [mHC]($text.id('component-mhc')) | Mixes residual streams before and after attention and expert computation. |
| [Paged caches]($text.id('component-paged-caches')) | Window KV uses FP8. Compressed KV and indexer keys use FP4. |

The [model compatibility catalog]($link.page('llmd/reference/model-compatibility')) lists the published launch recipes for this model.

# [Kernel implementations]($heading.id('kernels'))

The [kernel matrix]($text.id('component-kernels')) maps five operation families to eight platforms.
A cell names a platform-specific implementation or portable StableHLO. A dash marks an unspecified implementation.

- **[Attention.]($text.id('component-kernel-attention'))** Includes MLA and grouped-query attention.
- **[Indexer.]($text.id('component-kernel-indexer'))** Selects candidates for sparse attention. DSA and QSA are examples.
- **[Gated DeltaNet.]($text.id('component-kernel-deltanet'))** Six platforms list custom kernels. The Trainium and CPU entries are unspecified.
- **[Mixture of experts.]($text.id('component-kernel-experts'))** The matrix lists custom kernels for routed expert computation on NVIDIA, AMD, Google TPU, Apple and Furiosa. It lists StableHLO on the other three targets.
- **[mHC.]($text.id('component-kernel-mhc'))** Residual-stream mixing uses CuTe on NVIDIA, Fly on AMD and StableHLO on the other six targets.

| Platform | Attention | Indexer | Gated DeltaNet | Mixture of experts | mHC |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| NVIDIA | CuTe · Triton | Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe · Triton | CuTe |
| AMD | Fly · Triton | Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly · Triton | Fly |
| Google TPU | Mosaic | StableHLO | Mosaic | Mosaic | StableHLO |
| Trainium | NKI | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Intel | Triton | StableHLO | Triton | StableHLO | StableHLO |
| Apple | Metal | StableHLO | Metal | Metal | StableHLO |
| Furiosa | TCL | StableHLO | TCL | TCL | StableHLO |
| CPU | StableHLO | StableHLO | — | StableHLO | StableHLO |

## [Named kernel examples]($heading.id('named-kernel-examples'))

The compact map names DeepGEMM MegaMoE, FlashMLA, FlashAttention, FlashInfer,
Flash Linear Attention, DeepGEMM mHC and DeepSelect as examples of kernel implementations.

# [ZML compilation and execution]($heading.id('zml'))

[ZML]($text.id('component-zml')) compiles model code into an executable and loads weights into device buffers for that executable.

## [Express the model]($heading.id('zml-express-the-model'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-tensor-api">Tensor API</dt>
    <dd>Express matrix multiplication, gather, scan and sort in Zig. <code>q.dot(k, .hd).softmax(.k)</code> computes a dot product followed by softmax. <a href="../../../zml/learn/concepts/#tensor-bros">Tensors in ZML</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-sharding">Sharding</dt>
    <dd>Describe tensor placement on a device mesh with named axes so XLA can partition the computation. <a href="https://github.com/zml/zml/tree/master/examples/sharding">ZML sharding example</a></dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

## [Compile]($heading.id('zml-compile'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-compiler">Compiler</dt>
    <dd>Generates one MLIR program per shape bucket using dialects such as <code>stablehlo</code>, <code>sdy</code> and <code>func</code>.</dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-kernel-languages">Kernel languages</dt>
    <dd>Embed custom kernels in the graph. Examples include Triton, CuTe, CUDA Tile, FlyDSL, Mosaic, NKI, Metal and TCL.</dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

## [Load and run]($heading.id('zml-load-and-run'))

```=html
<dl class="architecture-reference-rows">
  <div>
    <dt id="component-io">I/O</dt>
    <dd>Read tensors from Hugging Face, S3, Google Cloud Storage, HTTP or disk, and transfer them between host and device using DMA. <a href="https://github.com/zml/zml/tree/master/examples/io">ZML I/O example</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-execution">Execution</dt>
    <dd>Launches compiled programs asynchronously with device buffers for parameters and inputs. <a href="../../../zml/learn/concepts/#model-lifecycle">ZML model lifecycle</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt id="component-pjrt">PJRT C API</dt>
    <dd>Provides a common plugin interface for compilation, data transfer and execution on each platform. <a href="https://github.com/openxla/xla">OpenXLA source</a></dd>
  </div>
</dl>
```

# [Platforms]($heading.id('platforms'))

The [framework targets]($text.id('component-platforms')) below show each platform’s runtime and kernel languages.
To deploy a server, check the [LLMD distributions]($link.page('llmd/reference/hardware-compatibility'))
and the [model recipes]($link.page('llmd/reference/model-compatibility')) for your hardware.

| Framework target | Runtime or architecture | Kernel languages shown |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [NVIDIA]($text.id('component-nvidia')) | CUDA | CuTe, CUDA Tile, Triton |
| [AMD]($text.id('component-amd')) | ROCm | FlyDSL, Triton |
| [Google TPU]($text.id('component-google-tpu')) | libtpu | Mosaic |
| [AWS Trainium]($text.id('component-trainium')) | Neuron | NKI |
| [Intel]($text.id('component-intel')) | oneAPI | Triton |
| [Apple]($text.id('component-apple')) | Metal | Metal |
| [Furiosa]($text.id('component-furiosa')) | Furiosa SDK | TCL |
| [CPU]($text.id('component-cpu')) | x86, Arm | — |
